.Dd August 4, 2026
.Dt T150CTL 1
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm t150ctl
.Nd set a Thrustmaster T150's range, gain and autocenter
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl v Ar vid
.Op Fl p Ar pid
.Cm range Ar degrees |
.Cm gain Ar n |
.Cm autocenter Ar n |
.Cm status
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
changes the settings the wheel keeps for itself: how far it turns lock to
lock, how hard it pushes overall, and how strongly it centres.
.Pp
It needs no privilege and it does not take the wheel away from anything else.
The device is opened without
.Dv kIOHIDOptionsTypeSeizeDevice ,
so a game running in a bottle keeps reading the wheel while this runs.
.Pp
The wheel stores these itself and keeps them until it is unplugged, so there
is nothing to leave running and no daemon involved. It does not read them
back either: the wheel publishes no report that carries them, so
.Cm status
describes the device rather than its settings.
.Pp
The wheel must be in firmware mode. If it is still at the boot product id,
run
.Xr t150boot 8
first.
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl v Ar vid
Vendor id. The default is 0x044f.
.It Fl p Ar pid
Product id. The default is 0xb677, the T150 in firmware mode.
.El
.Sh COMMANDS
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Cm range Ar degrees
Lock to lock, 270 to 1080. The firmware clamps anything smaller.
.Pp
For driving a game, this number and the game's own rotation setting must be
the same. DirectInput has no property for wheel rotation, so a game cannot
move the wheel and merely assumes it is already where its setting says, and if
the two disagree the car responds to a given movement of the hands by the
ratio between them. The T150 is 1080 and games often default to 900.
.Pp
Changing the game to 1080 is the easier way round and is the one that has been
tested. Setting the wheel here is for driving at a shorter range than the
wheel's own, and has not been tested under a running game.
.It Cm gain Ar n
Overall force, 0 to 10000. This scales every effect the wheel renders. It has
nothing to scale unless something is driving force feedback, so on an idle
wheel it does nothing you can feel.
.It Cm autocenter Ar n
Centring spring, 0 to 10000, where 0 releases the wheel.
.Pp
It is a progressive spring rather than a constant pull: the restoring torque
grows with displacement, so at low settings the wheel stiffens without
returning, and only from further out does it move back, in stages. Do not
expect a low setting to recentre it.
.It Cm status
What the wheel is, which mode it is in, and what its report descriptor
declares.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
It fails if no wheel matches, if the wheel cannot be opened, or if the wheel
refuses a packet.
.Sh EXAMPLES
Three quarters of a turn lock to lock, which is where most rally cars sit:
.Pp
.Dl $ t150ctl range 270
.Pp
Let go of the wheel completely:
.Pp
.Dl $ t150ctl autocenter 0
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr t150boot 8 ,
.Xr t150d 8 ,
.Xr crossover-wheel 7
.Sh CAVEATS
It refuses to guess between HID nodes. A wheel that publishes more than one
matching node is written to only when exactly one of them is a Generic
Desktop collection, because a set of nodes has no order and picking the first
would be a coin toss over which one drives the motor.
.Pp
A zero autocenter is the only thing that releases the wheel. The protocol has
a separate enable flag, and clearing it releases nothing at all, because the
autocenter is active whenever no application has the wheel's input open and
nothing on macOS opens one. This tool sends both, force first.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An Renaud Allard
